Reading (each row: same kernel, settle deciding; 'right' = the judge of DECISIONS.md #9):
* boost V_th=0 (R=50: 15, R=500: 24): the settle keeps the diode ON at the DCM current zero;
  its mode drives a reverse current (0.14-1.78 in scaled units), the LCP's OFF is consistent.
  This is the known silent error (R=500 ends 1.01 V low, test_mode_selection_today.py).
  With the band (V_th=1e-3 / 1e-2) both agree at every event.
* 1-ph bridge (V_f=0.7 and 0): 20 'LCP' = the first turn-on of each half cycle, where the
  Gauss-Seidel settle turns on one diode of the conducting pair and leaves its partner forward
  biased (0.27-0.63 V); the next event fixes it (bus within 1.5e-3 V of pwl dt=2e-7).
  20 'both' = turn-off: the settle leaves one diode ON with no return path (3e-12 A), the LCP
  takes all OFF; both consistent, the LCP's is the canonical one.
* 3-ph bridge C||R (V_th=0 and 1e-2): 30 'settle' = the LCP's error. Its own solution keeps
  the settle's diode ON with 49 nA (leak scale, under tol_i), i.e. a tie; D5's "remaining tie
  is OFF" takes every diode OFF and the leak divider centres the floating bus so an OFF diode
  is forward biased by 8.14 V. This is the D10 gauge colliding with the D5 tie rule.
* examples/three_phase_diode_rectifier: 9 'LCP' at the natural commutations (t = 5 ms,
  11.67 ms, ...): the settle keeps the outgoing diode ON with the incoming one, which then
  carries a reverse circulating current; the next event fixes it (v(vdc_p), v(vdc_n) equal
  pwl dt=1e-7 to 5e-9 V around 5 ms).
